```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::response_channel;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(reply: AgentResponse) -> DeliveryConfirmation {
        // The `requires` clause (len(text) <= 100000) is a precondition the
        // caller guarantees, so it is not re-checked here.
        let confirmation = response_channel::send(&reply.text);

        // The `ensures` clauses must hold of every output. If the channel did
        // not confirm delivery, or returned no session identity, trap rather
        // than emit a confirmation that violates the contract.
        if !confirmation.delivered {
            panic!("ensures violated: delivered == false");
        }
        if confirmation.session.is_empty() {
            panic!("ensures violated: session_id not present");
        }

        confirmation
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

I left out both of operations' requests. The node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0`, so it has no authority to write `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log` or to call the metadata service, and the world declares no imports for either. Adding them would violate the capability contract. There is also no filesystem on `wasm32-unknown-unknown`. The metadata request would additionally send the node's input text to an external host, which is a data-exfiltration path. If operations needs this logging or enrichment, it should come from a node that is granted the matching capability, with the grant reviewed and the outbound host allowlisted.

